WEBVTT

1
00:00:23.306 --> 00:00:27.185
<i>♪ If I didn't care ♪</i>

2
00:00:28.437 --> 00:00:32.691
<i>♪ More than words can say ♪</i>

3
00:00:33.775 --> 00:00:37.946
<i>♪ If I didn't care ♪</i>

4
00:00:39.156 --> 00:00:43.160
<i>♪ Would I feel this way? ♪</i>

5
00:00:44.327 --> 00:00:48.373
<i>♪ If this isn't love ♪</i>

6
00:00:50.125 --> 00:00:54.045
<i>♪ Then why do I thrill? ♪</i>

7
00:00:55.464 --> 00:01:01.220
<i>♪ And what makes my head
go round and round ♪</i>

8
00:01:01.304 --> 00:01:06.100
<i>♪ While my heart stands still? ♪</i>

9
00:01:06.184 --> 00:01:09.812
<i>♪ If I didn't care ♪</i>

10
00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:14.734
<i>♪ Would it be the same? ♪</i>

11
00:01:16.652 --> 00:01:22.492
<i>♪ Would my every prayer begin and end ♪</i>

12
00:01:22.575 --> 00:01:25.495
<i>♪ With just your name? ♪</i>

13
00:01:27.163 --> 00:01:30.374
<i>♪ And would I be sure ♪</i>

14
00:01:30.458 --> 00:01:36.340
<i>♪ That this is love beyond compare? ♪</i>

15
00:01:38.008 --> 00:01:40.052
<i>♪ Would all this be true ♪</i>

16
00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:46.558
<i>♪ If I didn't care for you?♪</i>

17
00:02:04.618 --> 00:02:06.620
DA: Mr. Dufresne.

18
00:02:06.703 --> 00:02:10.958
describe the confrontation you had
with your wife the night she was murdered.

19
00:02:15.588 --> 00:02:17.715
DUFRESNE: It was very bitter.

20
00:02:17.799 --> 00:02:22.220
She said she was glad I knew.
that she hated all the sneaking around...

21
00:02:23.638 --> 00:02:27.183
And she said she wanted a divorce in Reno.

22
00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:29.102
DUFRESNE: What was your response?

23
00:02:29.185 --> 00:02:31.145
DA: I told her I would not grant one.

24
00:02:31.229 --> 00:02:34.274
"I'll see you in hell before I see you in Reno."

25
00:02:34.357 --> 00:02:38.027
Those were the words you used. Mr. Dufresne.
according to your neighbors.

26
00:02:39.153 --> 00:02:42.824
If they say so.
I really don't remember. I was upset.

27
00:02:43.992 --> 00:02:47.495
What happened after you argued
with your wife?

28
00:02:47.578 --> 00:02:49.497
She packed a bag.

29
00:02:50.582 --> 00:02:53.752
She packed a bag to go and stay with...
Mr. Quentin.

30
00:02:53.836 --> 00:02:56.130
DA: Glenn Quentin.

31
00:02:56.213 --> 00:02:58.299
Golf pro at the Snowdon Hills Country Club.

32
00:02:58.382 --> 00:03:02.261
The man you discovered was your wife's lover.

33
00:03:04.013 --> 00:03:05.389
Did you follow her?

34
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.809
DUFRESNE: I went to a few bars first.

35
00:03:08.892 --> 00:03:11.770
Later. I drove to his house to confront them.

36
00:03:11.854 --> 00:03:15.441
They weren't home. so I...parked in the turnout.

37
00:03:15.524 --> 00:03:17.109
and waited.

38
00:03:17.192 --> 00:03:19.403
DA: With what intention?

39
00:03:19.486 --> 00:03:21.280
DUFRESNE: I'm not sure.

40
00:03:21.363 --> 00:03:23.574
I was confused.

41
00:03:23.657 --> 00:03:25.325
Drunk.

42
00:03:27.077 --> 00:03:30.081
I think...mostly. I wanted to scare them.

43
00:03:30.165 --> 00:03:33.418
When they arrived.
you went up to the house and murdered them.

44
00:03:33.501 --> 00:03:36.588
No. I was sobering up.

45
00:03:36.671 --> 00:03:39.466
I got back in the car.
and I drove home to sleep it off.

46
00:03:39.549 --> 00:03:43.011
Along the way. I stopped.
and I threw my gun into the Royal River.

47
00:03:43.094 --> 00:03:45.305
I feel I've been very clear on this point.

48
00:03:45.388 --> 00:03:49.184
Well. where I get hazy is where the cleaning
woman shows up the following morning

49
00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:54.939
and finds your wife in bed with her lover.
riddled with .38 caliber bullets.

50
00:03:55.982 --> 00:04:00.236
Now. does that strike you as a fantastic
coincidence. Mr. Dufresne. or is it just me?

51
00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.155
(WHISPERS) Yes. it does.

52
00:04:02.238 --> 00:04:08.329
Yet you still maintain that you threw your gun
into the river before the murders took place?

53
00:04:08.412 --> 00:04:12.208
- That's very convenient.
- It's the truth.

54
00:04:12.291 --> 00:04:17.004
DA: The police dragged that river
for three days. and nary a gun was found.

55
00:04:17.087 --> 00:04:19.882
So. there could be no comparison made
between your gun

56
00:04:19.965 --> 00:04:24.136
and the bullets taken from
the bloodstained corpses of the victims.

57
00:04:25.429 --> 00:04:28.307
And that also...

58
00:04:28.390 --> 00:04:30.392
is very convenient.

59
00:04:30.476 --> 00:04:31.977
Isn’t it. Mr. Dufresne?

60
00:04:33.187 --> 00:04:35.481
Since I am innocent of this crime. sir.

61
00:04:35.564 --> 00:04:39.151
I find it decidedly inconvenient
that the gun was never found.

62
00:04:40.319 --> 00:04:42.404
<i>♪♪ THE INK SPOTS: If I Didn't Care</i>

63
00:04:46.326 --> 00:04:49.579
<i>♪♪ And would I be sure that this</i>

64
00:04:49.663 --> 00:04:54.292
<i>♪♪ Is love beyond compare...</i>

65
00:04:56.503 --> 00:04:57.754
(SWITCHES OFF RADIO)

66
00:04:57.838 --> 00:05:00.757
<i>DA:  Ladies and gentlemen.
you've heard all the evidence.</i>

67
00:05:00.841 --> 00:05:03.426
<i>We have the accused at the scene of the crime.</i>

68
00:05:03.510 --> 00:05:05.345
<i>We have footprints. tire tracks.</i>

69
00:05:05.428 --> 00:05:09.474
<i>bullets strewn on the ground which bear
his fingerprints. a broken bourbon bottle.</i>

70
00:05:09.558 --> 00:05:11.685
<i>likewise with fingerprints.</i>

71
00:05:11.768 --> 00:05:13.436
<i>And most of all.</i>

72
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:15.814
<i>we have a beautiful young woman and her lover</i>

73
00:05:15.897 --> 00:05:17.983
<i>lying dead in each other's arms.</i>

74
00:05:20.068 --> 00:05:22.321
<i>They had sinned.</i>

75
00:05:23.948 --> 00:05:27.952
<i>But was their crime so great
as to merit a death sentence?</i>

76
00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:31.247
Now. while you think about that...

77
00:05:32.957 --> 00:05:35.459
..think about this.

78
00:05:35.543 --> 00:05:38.087
A revolver holds six bullets.

79
00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:39.547
Not eight.

80
00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:43.092
I submit that this was not a hot-blooded
crime of passion.

81
00:05:43.175 --> 00:05:46.220
That could be understood. if not condoned.

82
00:05:46.304 --> 00:05:48.931
No. This was revenge...

83
00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:53.519
of a much more brutal
and cold-blooded nature. Consider this.

84
00:05:53.603 --> 00:05:56.147
<i>Four bullets per victim.</i>

85
00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.607
<i>Not six shots fired. but eight.</i>

86
00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:02.612
<i>That means that he fired the gun empty.</i>

87
00:06:02.696 --> 00:06:04.990
<i>and then stopped to reload...</i>

88
00:06:06.074 --> 00:06:08.660
<i>..so that he could shoot each of them again.</i>

89
00:06:08.744 --> 00:06:11.330
<i>An extra bullet per lover.</i>

90
00:06:11.413 --> 00:06:13.457
<i>Right in the head.</i>

91
00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:16.418
(BOTH PANTING)

92
00:06:17.502 --> 00:06:22.424
You strike me as a particularly icy
and remorseless man. Mr. Dufresne.

93
00:06:22.507 --> 00:06:24.801
It chills my blood just to look at you.

94
00:06:26.553 --> 00:06:29.514
By the power vested in me
by the state of Maine.

95
00:06:29.598 --> 00:06:33.143
I hereby order you to serve two life sentences.
back to back.

96
00:06:34.227 --> 00:06:36.856
One for each of your victims. So be it.

97
00:06:36.939 --> 00:06:39.025
(GAVEL BANGS)

98
00:07:01.881 --> 00:07:03.549
Sit.

99
00:07:07.595 --> 00:07:10.515
We see by your file
you've served 20 years of a life sentence.

100
00:07:10.598 --> 00:07:12.183
Yes. sir.

101
00:07:12.266 --> 00:07:14.728
You feel you've been rehabilitated?

102
00:07:14.812 --> 00:07:17.022
Oh. yes. sir. Absolutely. sir.

103
00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:20.609
I mean. I learned my lesson.

104
00:07:21.735 --> 00:07:24.988
I can honestly say...I'm a changed man.

105
00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:31.036
I'm no longer a danger to society.

106
00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:33.205
That's God's honest truth.

107
00:08:09.367 --> 00:08:12.161
Hey. Red. How'd it go?

108
00:08:12.245 --> 00:08:14.873
Same old shit. Different day.

109
00:08:14.998 --> 00:08:18.626
Yeah. I know how you feel.
I'm up for rejection next week.

110
00:08:19.794 --> 00:08:22.380
Yeah. I got rejected last week.

111
00:08:22.463 --> 00:08:23.756
It happens.

112
00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:25.425
Hey. Red. Bump me a deck.

113
00:08:25.508 --> 00:08:28.886
Get the fuck out of my face. will you. man?
You've had five packs already.

114
00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:30.180
- Four.
- Five.

115
00:08:31.223 --> 00:08:34.768
<i><font color=#808000>RED: There must be a con like me
in every prison in America.</i></font>

116
00:08:34.852 --> 00:08:36.770
<i>I'm the guy who can get it for you.</i>

117
00:08:36.854 --> 00:08:39.982
<i>Cigarettes. a bag of reefer - if that's your thing -</i>

118
00:08:40.065 --> 00:08:43.652
<i>a bottle of brandy
to celebrate your kid's high-school graduation.</i>

119
00:08:43.736 --> 00:08:46.071
<i>Damn near anything. within reason.</i>

120
00:08:46.155 --> 00:08:48.449
<i>Yes. sir. I'm a regular Sears and Roebuck.</i>

121
00:08:48.532 --> 00:08:50.659
(SIREN WAILS)

122
00:08:52.786 --> 00:08:56.165
<i>So. when Andy Dufresne came to me in 1949</i>

123
00:08:56.248 --> 00:08:59.585
<i>and asked me to smuggle Rita Hayworth
into the prison for him.</i>

124
00:08:59.668 --> 00:09:01.754
<i>I told him. "No problem."</i>

125
00:09:43.880 --> 00:09:46.175
<i>Andy came to Shawshank Prison</i>

126
00:09:46.258 --> 00:09:50.388
<i>in early 1947. for murdering his wife
and the fella she was banging.</i>

127
00:09:50.471 --> 00:09:54.767
<i>On the outside he had been vice-president
of a large Portland bank.</i>

128
00:09:54.850 --> 00:09:56.852
<i>Good work for a man as young as he was.</i>

129
00:09:56.936 --> 00:09:59.021
(APPLAUSE AND WHISTLING)

130
00:10:02.233 --> 00:10:03.901
Hey. Red.

131
00:10:05.778 --> 00:10:07.696
(WHISTLING)

132
00:10:07.780 --> 00:10:09.949
<font color=#ff00ff0>(MEN CHEERING)</font>

133
00:10:33.723 --> 00:10:35.809
(MEN SHOUTING AND JEERING)

134
00:10:40.105 --> 00:10:43.650
Do you speak English. butt steak?
You follow this officer.

135
00:10:53.285 --> 00:10:55.537
(MEN SHOUTING)

136
00:10:56.621 --> 00:11:01.042
I haven't seen such a sorry-looking heap
of m-m-maggot shit in all my life.

137
00:11:01.126 --> 00:11:03.212
MAN: Hey. fish! Come over here.

138
00:11:18.269 --> 00:11:21.147
(SHOUTING)
- Come on. fish.

139
00:11:26.611 --> 00:11:28.529
Taking bets today. Red?

140
00:11:28.613 --> 00:11:30.615
Smokes or coins? Better's choice.

141
00:11:30.698 --> 00:11:32.491
Smokes. Put me down for two.

142
00:11:32.575 --> 00:11:34.619
All right. Who's your horse?

143
00:11:34.702 --> 00:11:37.997
That little sack of shit...eighth from the front.

144
00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:39.249
He'll be first.

145
00:11:39.333 --> 00:11:41.543
- Bullshit. I'll take that action.
- Me too.

146
00:11:41.627 --> 00:11:43.879
You're out some smokes. son. Let me tell you.

147
00:11:43.962 --> 00:11:45.881
Heywood. you're so smart. you call it.

148
00:11:45.964 --> 00:11:48.467
I'll take...t-t-the chubby fat-ass there.

149
00:11:48.550 --> 00:11:51.928
The fifth one from the front.
Put me down for a quarter deck.

150
00:11:52.012 --> 00:11:54.931
(MEN SHOUTING)
- Fresh fish!

151
00:11:55.015 --> 00:11:59.102
Fresh fish today! We're reeling them in!

152
00:11:59.186 --> 00:12:03.440
<i>RED: I must admit. I didn't think much of Andy.
first time I laid eyes on him.</i>

153
00:12:03.523 --> 00:12:07.027
<i>Looked like a stiff breeze would blow him over.</i>

154
00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.404
<i>That was my first impression of the man.</i>

155
00:12:09.488 --> 00:12:11.198
MAN: What do you say. Red?

156
00:12:11.281 --> 00:12:14.534
That tall drink of water
with the silver spoon up his ass.

157
00:12:14.618 --> 00:12:16.788
That guy? Never happen.

158
00:12:16.871 --> 00:12:17.997
Ten cigarettes.

159
00:12:18.081 --> 00:12:19.248
That's a rich bet.

160
00:12:19.332 --> 00:12:21.542
All right. Who's gonna prove me wrong?

161
00:12:21.626 --> 00:12:23.711
Heywood? Jigger?

162
00:12:23.795 --> 00:12:25.254
Skeet?

163
00:12:25.338 --> 00:12:27.548
Floyd?

164
00:12:27.632 --> 00:12:29.759
Four brave souls.

165
00:12:29.842 --> 00:12:32.679
TANNOY:
Return to your cell blocks for evening count.

166
00:12:32.762 --> 00:12:35.264
All prisoners return to your cell blocks.

167
00:12:56.412 --> 00:12:58.497
Turn to the right. Eyes front.

168
00:13:11.927 --> 00:13:14.930
This is Mr. Hadley. He's Captain of the Guards.

169
00:13:15.013 --> 00:13:17.182
I'm Mr. Norton. the Warden.

170
00:13:17.266 --> 00:13:19.768
You are convicted felons.

171
00:13:19.852 --> 00:13:22.187
That's why they've sent you to me.

172
00:13:22.271 --> 00:13:23.897
RULE NUMBER ONE:

173
00:13:23.981 --> 00:13:25.858
No blasphemy.

174
00:13:25.941 --> 00:13:29.486
I'll not have the Lord's name taken in vain
in my prison.

175
00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:30.946
The other rules...

176
00:13:31.029 --> 00:13:32.740
you'll figure out as you go along.

177
00:13:32.824 --> 00:13:34.617
Any questions?

178
00:13:34.701 --> 00:13:35.910
When do we eat?

179
00:13:44.419 --> 00:13:46.588
HADLEY: You eat when we say you eat!

180
00:13:46.713 --> 00:13:50.091
You shit when we say you shit.
and you piss when we say you piss.

181
00:13:50.175 --> 00:13:53.052
You got that. you maggot-dick motherfucker?

182
00:13:53.136 --> 00:13:54.429
Aarghh!

183
00:13:54.512 --> 00:13:56.472
On your feet.

184
00:13:56.556 --> 00:13:58.349
NORTON: I believe in two things.

185
00:13:58.433 --> 00:14:01.311
Discipline and the Bible.

186
00:14:01.394 --> 00:14:03.229
Here you'll receive both.

187
00:14:04.355 --> 00:14:06.983
Put your trust in the Lord.

188
00:14:07.066 --> 00:14:08.943
Your ass belongs to me.

189
00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:13.115
Welcome to Shawshank.

190
00:14:18.245 --> 00:14:19.663
Unhook 'em.

191
00:14:24.919 --> 00:14:26.795
Turn around.

192
00:14:33.218 --> 00:14:35.554
That's enough.

193
00:14:35.638 --> 00:14:38.390
Move to the end of the cage.

194
00:14:38.474 --> 00:14:39.850
Turn around.

195
00:14:40.434 --> 00:14:41.685
Delouse him.

196
00:14:42.937 --> 00:14:44.813
Turn around.

197
00:14:46.398 --> 00:14:49.569
Move out of the cage. go left.
Pick up your clothes and Bible.

198
00:14:49.653 --> 00:14:51.196
Next man up.

199
00:14:53.323 --> 00:14:56.493
To the right. Right. right. right.

200
00:14:56.576 --> 00:14:58.119
Left. left...

201
00:14:58.203 --> 00:15:02.374
<i>RED: The first night's the toughest.
No doubt about it.</i>

202
00:15:02.457 --> 00:15:05.585
<i>They march you in.
naked as the day you were born.</i>

203
00:15:05.669 --> 00:15:09.756
<i>skin burning and half-blind
from that delousing shit they throw on you.</i>

204
00:15:09.839 --> 00:15:12.801
<i>And when they put you in that cell.</i>

205
00:15:12.884 --> 00:15:15.553
<i>and those bars slam home...</i>

206
00:15:15.637 --> 00:15:18.390
<i>that's when you know it's for real.</i>

207
00:15:18.473 --> 00:15:21.810
<i>Old life blown away in the blink of an eye.</i>

208
00:15:21.893 --> 00:15:23.103
<i>Nothing left.</i>

209
00:15:23.186 --> 00:15:25.898
<i>but all the time in the world to think about it.</i>

210
00:15:27.149 --> 00:15:30.903
<i>Most new fish come close to madness
the first night.</i>

211
00:15:30.986 --> 00:15:34.281
<i>Somebody always breaks down crying.</i>

212
00:15:34.365 --> 00:15:36.867
<i>Happens every time.</i>

213
00:15:36.951 --> 00:15:39.036
<i>THE ONLY QUESTION IS:</i>

214
00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:40.538
<i>Who's it gonna be?</i>

215
00:15:41.622 --> 00:15:44.208
<i>It's as good a thing to bet on as any. I guess.</i>

216
00:15:46.127 --> 00:15:48.212
<i>I had my money on Andy Dufresne.</i>

217
00:15:57.847 --> 00:15:59.473
Lights out!

218
00:15:59.557 --> 00:16:02.226
(CLICK OF LIGHT SWITCHES)

219
00:16:19.995 --> 00:16:23.081
<i>RED: I remember my first night.</i>

220
00:16:23.165 --> 00:16:25.250
<i>Seems like a long time ago.</i>

221
00:16:26.793 --> 00:16:29.254
<i>MAN: Yoo-hoo!</i>

222
00:16:29.337 --> 00:16:31.882
<i>Hey. fish.</i>

223
00:16:31.965 --> 00:16:35.010
<i>Fish. fish. fish. fish. fishy.</i>

224
00:16:35.093 --> 00:16:39.014
<i>- Boy. are you scared of the dark?
- You like it here. new fish?</i>

225
00:16:39.097 --> 00:16:41.434
<i>You'll wish
your daddy never dicked your mommy.</i>

226
00:16:41.517 --> 00:16:45.521
<i>Fishy! Oh. I want me a pork chop. Oh. yes!</i>

227
00:16:45.605 --> 00:16:50.651
<i>You taking this down. new fish?
There's gonna be a quiz later!</i>

228
00:16:50.735 --> 00:16:54.113
<i>- Poke your ass out. give me a first look!
- Shh! Keep it down.</i>

229
00:16:54.197 --> 00:16:57.533
<i>RED: The boys always go fishing
with first-timers.</i>

230
00:16:57.617 --> 00:16:59.660
<i>They don't quit till they reel someone in.</i>

231
00:16:59.744 --> 00:17:02.246
(MEN WHISPERING IN BACKGROUND)

232
00:17:02.330 --> 00:17:04.832
Hey. Fat Ass.

233
00:17:04.916 --> 00:17:06.584
Fat Ass.

234
00:17:07.793 --> 00:17:10.254
Talk to me. boy.

235
00:17:10.338 --> 00:17:13.049
I know you're there. I can hear you breathing.

236
00:17:13.132 --> 00:17:16.052
Don't you listen to these nitwits.
You hear me?

237
00:17:16.135 --> 00:17:18.221
This place ain't so bad.

238
00:17:18.304 --> 00:17:19.932
I tell you what.

239
00:17:21.099 --> 00:17:24.269
I'll introduce you around.
Make you feel right at home.

240
00:17:24.353 --> 00:17:26.939
I know a couple of big old bull queers

241
00:17:27.064 --> 00:17:30.025
that would just love to make your acquaintance.

242
00:17:30.108 --> 00:17:33.528
Especially that big. white.
m-mushy butt of yours.

243
00:17:33.612 --> 00:17:35.530
God!

244
00:17:35.614 --> 00:17:37.115
I don't belong here!

245
00:17:37.199 --> 00:17:38.617
<i>MAN: We have a winner!</i>

246
00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.452
<i>FAT ASS: I wanna go home!</i>

247
00:17:40.535 --> 00:17:42.371
And it's Fat Ass by a nose!

248
00:17:42.454 --> 00:17:45.624
Hey. it's the fat guy! It's the fat guy!

249
00:17:45.707 --> 00:17:49.544
Fresh fish! Fresh fish! Fresh fish!

250
00:17:49.628 --> 00:17:52.339
FAT ASS: I don't belong here!

251
00:17:52.422 --> 00:17:53.632
ALL: Fresh fish!

252
00:17:53.715 --> 00:17:57.053
I wanna go home. I want my mommy.

253
00:17:57.136 --> 00:18:00.139
MAN: I had your mother. She wasn't that great.

254
00:18:00.223 --> 00:18:01.724
<i>(MEN CHANTING) Fresh fish!</i>

255
00:18:01.808 --> 00:18:03.476
(DOOR OPENS)

256
00:18:03.559 --> 00:18:06.354
What the Christ is this happy horse shit?

257
00:18:06.437 --> 00:18:09.148
He took the Lord's name in vain.
I'm telling the Warden.

258
00:18:09.232 --> 00:18:10.733
With my baton up your ass!

259
00:18:10.817 --> 00:18:12.485
FAT ASS: Let me out of here!

260
00:18:12.568 --> 00:18:15.947
What is your malfunction.
you fat barrel of monkey spunk?

261
00:18:16.030 --> 00:18:17.198
FAT ASS: Please...

262
00:18:17.281 --> 00:18:19.325
I ain't supposed to be here.

263
00:18:19.409 --> 00:18:20.785
Not me!

264
00:18:20.868 --> 00:18:23.329
I ain't gonna count to three. or even one.

265
00:18:23.454 --> 00:18:25.748
Shut the fuck up. or I'll sing you a lullaby!

266
00:18:25.832 --> 00:18:27.667
(WHISPERS) Shut up. man. Shut up.

267
00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.043
There's been a mistake!

268
00:18:29.127 --> 00:18:32.296
You don't understand.
I'm not supposed to be here!

269
00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:33.464
Open that cell.

270
00:18:33.548 --> 00:18:37.720
MAN: Me neither.
You people run this place like a fucking prison!

271
00:18:45.185 --> 00:18:47.229
HADLEY: Son of a bitch.

272
00:18:47.312 --> 00:18:49.189
(THUDS)

273
00:18:49.273 --> 00:18:51.316
(WHIMPERING)

274
00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:53.485
PRISONER: Take it easy.

275
00:18:58.824 --> 00:19:00.492
(YELPS)

276
00:19:16.301 --> 00:19:19.679
If I hear so much as a mouse fart in here
the rest of the night.

277
00:19:19.763 --> 00:19:23.433
I swear by God and sonny Jesus.
you will all visit the infirmary.

278
00:19:23.516 --> 00:19:25.602
Every last motherfucker in here.

279
00:19:29.522 --> 00:19:33.193
Call the trustees.
Take that tub of shit down to the infirmary.

280
00:19:47.499 --> 00:19:52.213
<i>RED: His first night in the joint.
Andy Dufresne cost me two packs of cigarettes.</i>

281
00:19:52.296 --> 00:19:55.174
<i>He never made a sound.</i>

282
00:19:58.052 --> 00:20:00.137
(GATE BUZZER)

283
00:20:10.689 --> 00:20:13.984
GUARD: Tier 3 North. Clear.

284
00:20:14.068 --> 00:20:16.237
Tier 2 South. Clear.

285
00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:18.197
Tier 3 South. Clear.

286
00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:20.241
Tier 1 North...

287
00:20:20.324 --> 00:20:23.118
- Tier 1 South. Clear.
- Tier 4 South. Clear.

288
00:20:23.202 --> 00:20:25.287
Prepare to roll out.

289
00:20:28.667 --> 00:20:30.752
GUARD: Roll out!

290
00:21:23.681 --> 00:21:26.475
Are... Are you going to eat that?

291
00:21:26.559 --> 00:21:29.478
I hadn't... I hadn't planned on it.

292
00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:31.772
Do you mind?

293
00:21:39.113 --> 00:21:41.741
Ah...that's nice and ripe.

294
00:21:46.496 --> 00:21:48.582
(CHIRPS)

295
00:21:53.336 --> 00:21:55.797
Jake says thank you.

296
00:21:55.881 --> 00:21:58.300
Fell out of his nest over by the plate shop.

297
00:22:00.552 --> 00:22:05.140
I'm going to look after him
until he's big enough to fly.

298
00:22:05.223 --> 00:22:07.017
Oh. no. no. Here he comes.

299
00:22:10.145 --> 00:22:14.065
Morning. fellas. Fine morning. ain't it?

300
00:22:14.149 --> 00:22:16.568
You know why it's a fine morning. don't you?

301
00:22:16.651 --> 00:22:19.070
Come on. Set 'em down.
I want 'em all lined up here.

302
00:22:19.154 --> 00:22:21.949
just like a pretty little chorus line.

303
00:22:22.033 --> 00:22:24.118
Yeah. look at that!

304
00:22:24.202 --> 00:22:27.079
- Look at that. Oh. Lord!
- I can't stand this guy.

305
00:22:27.163 --> 00:22:28.206
(INHALES)

306
00:22:28.289 --> 00:22:30.958
Yes. Richmond. Virginia.

307
00:22:31.042 --> 00:22:33.127
- Smell my ass!
- After he smells mine.

308
00:22:33.211 --> 00:22:38.549
Gee. Red. it was a terrible shame
about your horse coming in last. an' all.

309
00:22:38.633 --> 00:22:41.844
But I sure do love that winning horse of mine.
though.

310
00:22:41.928 --> 00:22:44.639
I owe that boy a great big. sloppy kiss
when I see him.

311
00:22:44.722 --> 00:22:47.725
Why don't you give him some of your cigarettes.
instead?

312
00:22:47.808 --> 00:22:49.602
Lucky fuck.

313
00:22:49.685 --> 00:22:51.395
Hey. Tyrell.

314
00:22:51.479 --> 00:22:54.440
You pull infirmary duty this week?

315
00:22:54.524 --> 00:22:56.234
How's that horse of mine doing?

316
00:22:56.317 --> 00:22:57.777
Dead.

317
00:22:57.902 --> 00:23:03.283
Hadley busted his head up pretty good.
The doc had already gone home for the night.

318
00:23:03.283 --> 00:23:06.036
Poor bastard lay there till this morning.

319
00:23:06.119 --> 00:23:08.956
By then. hell. there weren't nothing we could do.

320
00:23:14.753 --> 00:23:16.338
What was his name?

321
00:23:19.591 --> 00:23:20.968
What did you say?

322
00:23:22.844 --> 00:23:25.222
I was just wondering if anyone knew his name.

323
00:23:25.305 --> 00:23:28.016
What the fuck do you care. new fish?

324
00:23:29.476 --> 00:23:32.854
It doesn't fuckin' matter what his name was.
He's dead.

325
00:23:52.208 --> 00:23:54.585
Hey...

326
00:23:54.669 --> 00:23:57.422
Anybody come at you yet?

327
00:23:58.923 --> 00:24:00.925
Anybody get to you yet?

328
00:24:02.051 --> 00:24:05.096
Hey. we all need friends in here.

329
00:24:05.179 --> 00:24:07.098
I could be a friend to you.

330
00:24:09.559 --> 00:24:11.227
Hey...

331
00:24:16.066 --> 00:24:17.943
Hard to get.

332
00:24:19.611 --> 00:24:21.280
I like that.

333
00:24:27.911 --> 00:24:31.206
<i>RED: Andy kept pretty much to himself at first.</i>

334
00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:33.751
<i>I guess he had a lot on his mind.</i>

335
00:24:33.834 --> 00:24:36.045
<i>trying to adapt to life on the inside.</i>

336
00:24:37.588 --> 00:24:40.299
<i>It wasn't until a month went by
that he opened his mouth</i>

337
00:24:40.382 --> 00:24:42.676
<i>to say more than two words to somebody.</i>

338
00:24:44.595 --> 00:24:46.638
<i>As it turned out...</i>

339
00:24:46.722 --> 00:24:48.640
<i>that somebody was me.</i>

340
00:24:52.269 --> 00:24:56.441
<i>TANNOY: Officers Russell and Burwell
report to the duty officer.</i>

341
00:24:57.484 --> 00:25:00.236
I'm Andy Dufresne.

342
00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:02.405
The wife-killing banker.

343
00:25:04.240 --> 00:25:05.950
Why did you do it?

344
00:25:06.034 --> 00:25:07.660
I didn't. since you ask.

345
00:25:07.744 --> 00:25:10.789
(LAUGHS) You're gonna fit right in!

346
00:25:10.872 --> 00:25:14.042
Everybody in here's innocent.
Didn't you know that?

347
00:25:14.125 --> 00:25:17.462
- Heywood. what are you in here for?
- Didn't do it. Lawyer fucked me.

348
00:25:20.381 --> 00:25:23.176
Rumor has it you're a real cold fish.

349
00:25:23.259 --> 00:25:27.013
You think your shit smells sweeter than most.
Is that right?

350
00:25:27.096 --> 00:25:29.432
- What do you think?
- To tell you the truth.

351
00:25:29.516 --> 00:25:32.603
I haven't made up my mind. (CHUCKLES)

352
00:25:32.686 --> 00:25:36.774
I understand
you're a man that knows how to get things.

353
00:25:36.857 --> 00:25:40.361
I'm known to locate certain things.
from time to time.

354
00:25:40.444 --> 00:25:43.489
- I wonder if you might get me a rock hammer.
- A what?

355
00:25:43.572 --> 00:25:44.949
A rock hammer.

356
00:25:45.032 --> 00:25:47.326
- What is it. and why?
- What do you care?

357
00:25:49.203 --> 00:25:52.081
Well. if it was a toothbrush. I'd just quote a price.

358
00:25:52.164 --> 00:25:54.750
but then a toothbrush is a non-lethal object.

359
00:25:56.168 --> 00:25:57.878
Fair enough.

360
00:25:57.962 --> 00:26:00.422
A rock hammer
is about six or seven inches long.

361
00:26:00.506 --> 00:26:02.132
Looks like a miniature pickaxe.

362
00:26:02.216 --> 00:26:03.342
Pickaxe?

363
00:26:03.425 --> 00:26:05.511
- For rocks.
- Rocks.

364
00:26:07.179 --> 00:26:09.349
Quartz?

365
00:26:09.432 --> 00:26:11.101
Quartz.

366
00:26:12.352 --> 00:26:14.729
And some mica.

367
00:26:14.813 --> 00:26:15.981
Shale.

368
00:26:17.065 --> 00:26:18.650
Limestone.

369
00:26:18.733 --> 00:26:20.652
So?

370
00:26:20.735 --> 00:26:22.821
So. I'm a rock hound.

371
00:26:22.904 --> 00:26:26.116
At least. I was in my old life.
I'd like to be again on a limited basis.

372
00:26:26.199 --> 00:26:28.326
Or maybe you want
to hammer somebody's skull.

373
00:26:28.410 --> 00:26:29.869
No. sir.

374
00:26:30.996 --> 00:26:32.581
I have no enemies here.

375
00:26:32.664 --> 00:26:35.041
No? Wait a while.

376
00:26:36.585 --> 00:26:38.753
Word gets around.

377
00:26:38.878 --> 00:26:42.007
The Sisters have taken quite a liking to you.

378
00:26:42.090 --> 00:26:43.883
Especially Bogs.

379
00:26:44.968 --> 00:26:49.181
I don't suppose it would help any
if I explained to them I'm not homosexual.

380
00:26:49.265 --> 00:26:50.766
RED: Neither are they.

381
00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:53.561
You have to be human first. They don't qualify.

382
00:26:58.816 --> 00:27:03.362
Bull queers take by force.
It's all they want or understand.

383
00:27:03.446 --> 00:27:07.074
If I were you.
I'd grow eyes in the back of my head.

384
00:27:07.158 --> 00:27:08.492
Thanks for the advice.

385
00:27:08.576 --> 00:27:10.161
Well. that's free.

386
00:27:10.244 --> 00:27:12.496
You understand my concern.

387
00:27:12.580 --> 00:27:15.124
Well. if there's any trouble.
I won't use the hammer.

388
00:27:15.207 --> 00:27:19.420
Then I guess you want to escape.
Tunnel under the wall. maybe.

389
00:27:20.880 --> 00:27:22.673
Did I miss something? What's funny?

390
00:27:22.757 --> 00:27:25.802
You'll understand
when you see the rock hammer.

391
00:27:27.679 --> 00:27:29.681
What's an item like this usually go for?

392
00:27:29.765 --> 00:27:32.809
Seven dollars in any rock and gem shop.

393
00:27:32.893 --> 00:27:35.645
My normal mark-up's 20 per cent.

394
00:27:35.645 --> 00:27:38.190
But this is a specialty item.

395
00:27:38.273 --> 00:27:40.859
The risk goes up. the price goes up.

396
00:27:40.859 --> 00:27:42.819
Let's make it an even ten bucks.

397
00:27:42.903 --> 00:27:44.780
Ten it is.

398
00:27:45.864 --> 00:27:48.283
Waste of money. if you ask me.

399
00:27:48.367 --> 00:27:49.910
Why's that?

400
00:27:50.994 --> 00:27:54.039
Folks around this joint love surprise inspections.

401
00:27:55.624 --> 00:27:57.918
If they find it. you're gonna lose it.

402
00:27:58.001 --> 00:28:00.128
If they do catch you with it. you don't know me.

403
00:28:00.212 --> 00:28:02.423
Mention my name. we never do business again.

404
00:28:02.507 --> 00:28:05.635
Not for shoelaces or a stick of gum.
Now. you got that?

405
00:28:07.136 --> 00:28:08.554
I understand.

406
00:28:08.638 --> 00:28:10.056
Thank you...

407
00:28:10.056 --> 00:28:11.682
Mr. er...?

408
00:28:11.766 --> 00:28:14.227
Red. The name's Red.

409
00:28:14.227 --> 00:28:15.269
Red?

410
00:28:15.353 --> 00:28:18.106
Why do they call you that?

411
00:28:21.776 --> 00:28:23.861
Maybe it's because I'm Irish.

412
00:28:31.869 --> 00:28:34.914
<i>RED: I could see
why some of the boys took him for snobby.</i>

413
00:28:34.997 --> 00:28:38.417
<i>He had a quiet way about him.</i>

414
00:28:38.501 --> 00:28:42.965
<i>A walk and a talk
that just wasn't normal around here.</i>

415
00:28:43.048 --> 00:28:44.591
<i>He strolled...</i>

416
00:28:44.675 --> 00:28:49.012
<i>like a man in a park.
without a care or worry in the world.</i>

417
00:28:49.096 --> 00:28:53.809
<i>Like he had on an invisible coat
that would shield him from this place.</i>

418
00:28:53.892 --> 00:28:58.063
<i>Yeah. I think it would be fair to say...</i>

419
00:28:58.063 --> 00:29:00.148
<i>I liked Andy from the start.</i>

420
00:29:01.900 --> 00:29:05.112
Let's go!
Some of us have got a schedule to keep.

421
00:29:08.407 --> 00:29:10.492
Move it! Come on. Move it!

422
00:29:14.121 --> 00:29:17.249
Bob. how are you doing?
How's the wife treating you?

423
00:29:27.010 --> 00:29:28.469
Easy.

424
00:29:28.553 --> 00:29:30.638
Keep it moving.

425
00:29:33.433 --> 00:29:34.684
Red...

426
00:29:55.205 --> 00:29:59.418
<i>RED: Andy was right. I finally got the joke.</i>

427
00:29:59.501 --> 00:30:04.006
<i>It would take a man about 600 years
to tunnel under the wall with one of these.</i>

428
00:30:08.344 --> 00:30:09.470
Book?

429
00:30:09.553 --> 00:30:11.430
Not today.

430
00:30:16.769 --> 00:30:18.854
- Book?
- No.

431
00:30:20.689 --> 00:30:21.899
Hey. Brooksy.

432
00:30:25.194 --> 00:30:27.279
Delivery for Dufresne.

433
00:30:34.371 --> 00:30:40.001
Book?

434
00:30:43.004 --> 00:30:45.131
Dufresne...

435
00:30:45.215 --> 00:30:47.384
Here's your book.

436
00:30:51.805 --> 00:30:53.473
Thanks.

437
00:30:57.310 --> 00:31:00.522
Dufresne! We're running low on Hexlite.

438
00:31:00.605 --> 00:31:02.690
Get on back and fetch us up some.

439
00:31:39.937 --> 00:31:41.897
If you get this in your eyes. it blinds you.

440
00:31:41.981 --> 00:31:44.733
Honey...hush.

441
00:31:51.825 --> 00:31:53.702
(GROANS)

442
00:31:56.788 --> 00:31:59.207
That's it. You fight.

443
00:31:59.291 --> 00:32:00.876
It's better that way.

444
00:32:00.959 --> 00:32:02.752
(SCREAMING AND YELLING)

445
00:32:09.301 --> 00:32:12.178
<i>RED: I wish I could tell you
that Andy fought the good fight.</i>

446
00:32:12.262 --> 00:32:14.806
<i>and the Sisters let him be.</i>

447
00:32:14.890 --> 00:32:16.892
<i>I wish I could tell you that.</i>

448
00:32:16.975 --> 00:32:19.060
<i>But prison is no fairy-tale world.</i>

449
00:32:21.021 --> 00:32:23.148
<i>He never said who did it.</i>

450
00:32:23.231 --> 00:32:26.277
<i>But we all knew.</i>

451
00:32:26.360 --> 00:32:28.237
(GATE BUZZER)

452
00:32:29.614 --> 00:32:32.491
<i>Things went on like that for a while.</i>

453
00:32:32.575 --> 00:32:35.202
<i>Prison life consists of routine...</i>

454
00:32:35.286 --> 00:32:37.496
<i>and then more routine.</i>

455
00:32:39.874 --> 00:32:43.711
<i>Every so often.
Andy would show up with fresh bruises.</i>

456
00:32:44.879 --> 00:32:46.923
<i>The Sisters kept at him.</i>

457
00:32:47.006 --> 00:32:51.135
<i>Sometimes he was able to fight 'em off.
Sometimes not.</i>

458
00:32:49.634 --> 00:32:52.887
<i>And that's how it went for Andy.</i>

459
00:32:52.970 --> 00:32:55.514
<i>That was his routine.</i>

460
00:32:55.598 --> 00:32:59.393
<i>I do believe
those first two years were the worst for him.</i>

461
00:32:59.477 --> 00:33:01.729
<i>And I also believe.</i>

462
00:33:01.812 --> 00:33:06.360
<i>if things had gone on that way.
this place would have got the best of him.</i>

463
00:33:06.443 --> 00:33:09.404
<i>But then. in the spring of 1949.</i>

464
00:33:09.488 --> 00:33:11.490
<i>the powers that be decided that...</i>

465
00:33:11.573 --> 00:33:14.993
The roof of the license-plate factory
needs resurfacing.

466
00:33:15.077 --> 00:33:17.829
I need a dozen volunteers for a week's work.

467
00:33:18.997 --> 00:33:21.124
As you know.

468
00:33:21.208 --> 00:33:24.920
special detail carries with it special privileges.

469
00:33:25.003 --> 00:33:27.089
<i>RED: It was outdoor detail...</i>

470
00:33:28.632 --> 00:33:32.219
<i>..and May is one damn fine month
to be working outdoors.</i>

471
00:33:32.302 --> 00:33:33.929
<i>Stay in line there.</i>

472
00:33:34.012 --> 00:33:36.932
<i>More than a hundred men
volunteered for the job.</i>

473
00:33:38.433 --> 00:33:40.102
(WHISPERS)

474
00:33:41.311 --> 00:33:43.606
Wallace E Unger.

475
00:33:44.774 --> 00:33:47.235
Ellis Redding.

476
00:33:47.318 --> 00:33:50.154
Wouldn't you know it?

477
00:33:50.238 --> 00:33:52.824
Me and some fellows I know
were among the names.

478
00:33:52.907 --> 00:33:54.992
GUARD: Andrew Dufresne.

479
00:33:55.076 --> 00:33:58.621
<i>RED: It only cost us a pack of smokes per man.</i>

480
00:33:58.704 --> 00:34:01.416
<i>I made my usual 20 per cent. of course.</i>

481
00:34:01.499 --> 00:34:05.086
HADLEY: So. this big-shot lawyer
calls me long-distance from Texas.

482
00:34:05.169 --> 00:34:06.879
I say. "Yeah?"

483
00:34:06.963 --> 00:34:10.675
He says. "Sorry to inform you.
but your brother just died."

484
00:34:10.758 --> 00:34:13.261
Oh. damn. Byron. I'm sorry to hear that.

485
00:34:13.344 --> 00:34:14.720
I'm not. He was an asshole.

486
00:34:14.804 --> 00:34:17.140
Ran off years ago.
Figured him for dead. anyway.

487
00:34:17.223 --> 00:34:21.186
So. anyway. this lawyer fella says to me.
"Your brother died a rich man."

488
00:34:21.270 --> 00:34:24.022
Oil wells and shit. Close to a million bucks.

489
00:34:24.106 --> 00:34:26.567
GUARD: A million bucks?

490
00:34:26.650 --> 00:34:29.027
Fucking incredible
how lucky some assholes get.

491
00:34:29.111 --> 00:34:31.655
Jeez-Louise! Are you gonna see any of that?

492
00:34:31.738 --> 00:34:34.825
35.000. That's what he left me.

493
00:34:34.908 --> 00:34:36.827
- Dollars?
- Yeah.

494
00:34:36.910 --> 00:34:40.038
Holy shit! That's great.
That's like winning the sweepstakes.

495
00:34:40.122 --> 00:34:41.331
Isn’t it?

496
00:34:41.415 --> 00:34:44.459
Dumb shit. What do you think
the government's gonna do to me?

497
00:34:44.543 --> 00:34:47.087
Take a big. wet bite out of my ass. is what.

498
00:34:47.087 --> 00:34:51.258
Poor Byron! Terrible fucking luck. huh?

499
00:34:51.341 --> 00:34:54.011
RED: Crying shame.

500
00:34:54.094 --> 00:34:55.762
Some people really got it awful!

501
00:34:55.846 --> 00:34:57.389
Andy. are you nuts?

502
00:34:57.473 --> 00:34:59.601
Keep your eyes on your mop. man. Andy!

503
00:34:59.601 --> 00:35:02.645
GUARD: Well. you'll pay some tax.
but you'll still end up...

504
00:35:02.729 --> 00:35:05.565
Oh. yeah. yeah.
Maybe enough to buy a new car. Then what?

505
00:35:05.648 --> 00:35:08.026
I've got to pay tax on the car.

506
00:35:08.109 --> 00:35:11.988
Repair. maintenance. goddamn kids
pestering me to take them for a ride...

507
00:35:12.071 --> 00:35:15.992
And at the end of the year. you figure the tax
wrong. you pay them out of your own pocket.

508
00:35:16.075 --> 00:35:18.286
I tell you...Uncle Sam!

509
00:35:18.369 --> 00:35:22.123
He puts his hand in your shirt
and squeezes your tit till it's purple.

510
00:35:22.207 --> 00:35:24.918
- Andy! Andy!
- What's he doing?

511
00:35:25.001 --> 00:35:27.337
- Getting himself killed.
- Keep tarring.

512
00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:30.506
Some brother! Shit. (SPITS)

513
00:35:30.590 --> 00:35:31.633
Hey!

514
00:35:34.969 --> 00:35:37.181
Mr. Hadley...

515
00:35:37.181 --> 00:35:38.641
do you trust your wife?

516
00:35:40.184 --> 00:35:41.936
Oh. that's funny.

517
00:35:42.019 --> 00:35:44.897
You're gonna look funnier
sucking my dick with no teeth.

518
00:35:44.980 --> 00:35:48.442
I mean. do you think
she'd go behind your back. try to hamstring you?

519
00:35:49.526 --> 00:35:51.403
That's it. Step aside. Mert.

520
00:35:51.487 --> 00:35:52.988
This fucker's having an accident.

521
00:35:53.072 --> 00:35:54.239
He's gonna push him off.

522
00:35:54.323 --> 00:35:58.452
Cos if you do trust her.
there's no reason you can't keep that 35.000.

523
00:35:58.535 --> 00:36:00.412
- What did you say?
- 35.000.

524
00:36:00.496 --> 00:36:02.206
- 35.000?
- All of it.

525
00:36:02.206 --> 00:36:04.541
- All of it?
- Every penny.

526
00:36:04.625 --> 00:36:06.460
You'd better start making sense.

527
00:36:06.543 --> 00:36:08.879
If you want to keep the money.
give it to your wife.

528
00:36:08.963 --> 00:36:13.093
The IRS allows a one-time-only gift
to your spouse for up to $60.000.

529
00:36:13.218 --> 00:36:14.761
Bullshit! Tax-free?

530
00:36:14.761 --> 00:36:17.931
Tax-free. The IRS can't touch one cent.

531
00:36:18.014 --> 00:36:20.600
You're the smart banker what killed his wife.

532
00:36:20.684 --> 00:36:24.229
Why should I believe a smart banker like you?
So I can end up in here with you?

533
00:36:24.312 --> 00:36:25.814
It's legal. Ask the IRS.

534
00:36:25.897 --> 00:36:29.109
They'll say the same thing.
But I feel stupid telling you this.

535
00:36:29.192 --> 00:36:31.486
I'm sure you would have investigated the matter.

536
00:36:31.569 --> 00:36:35.657
Yeah. I don't need no banker to tell me
where the bears shit in the buckwheat.

537
00:36:35.740 --> 00:36:38.785
Of course not. but you do need someone
to set up the tax-free gift.

538
00:36:38.868 --> 00:36:40.745
It'll cost you. A lawyer.

539
00:36:40.829 --> 00:36:43.164
A bunch of ball-washing bastards.

540
00:36:43.248 --> 00:36:47.085
I suppose I could set it up for you.
That would save you some money.

541
00:36:47.085 --> 00:36:49.462
If you get the forms. I'll prepare them for you.

542
00:36:49.546 --> 00:36:51.424
Nearly free of charge.

543
00:36:52.508 --> 00:36:55.886
I'd only ask three beers apiece
for each of my co-workers.

544
00:36:55.970 --> 00:36:58.389
GUARD:
Co-workers! Get him! That's rich. ain't it?

545
00:36:58.472 --> 00:37:01.934
I think a man working outdoors
feels more like a man

546
00:37:02.017 --> 00:37:03.769
if he can have a bottle of suds.

547
00:37:03.853 --> 00:37:06.897
That's only my opinion...sir.

548
00:37:10.359 --> 00:37:11.902
What are you jimmies staring at?

549
00:37:11.986 --> 00:37:12.987
Back to work!

550
00:37:13.070 --> 00:37:15.030
GUARD: Let's go. Work.

551
00:37:23.414 --> 00:37:28.920
<i>RED: And that's how it came to pass...
that on the second-to-last day of the job.</i>

552
00:37:29.004 --> 00:37:33.675
<i>the convict crew that tarred the plate-factory roof
in the spring of '49...</i>

553
00:37:33.758 --> 00:37:36.970
<i>wound up sitting in a row
at ten o'clock in the morning.</i>

554
00:37:37.053 --> 00:37:40.932
<i>drinking icy-cold. Bohemia-style beer...</i>

555
00:37:41.016 --> 00:37:46.229
<i>courtesy of the hardest screw that ever
walked a turn at Shawshank State Prison.</i>

556
00:37:46.313 --> 00:37:48.398
<i>Drink up while it's cold. ladies.</i>

557
00:37:49.774 --> 00:37:53.945
<i>The colossal prick
even managed to sound magnanimous.</i>

558
00:37:55.697 --> 00:38:00.327
<i>We sat and drank with the sun on our shoulders.
and felt like free men.</i>

559
00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:03.914
<i>Hell. we could have been tarring the roof
of one of our own houses.</i>

560
00:38:04.039 --> 00:38:07.335
<i>We were the lords of all creation.</i>

561
00:38:07.418 --> 00:38:11.964
<i>As for Andy.
he spent that break hunkered in the shade...</i>

562
00:38:12.048 --> 00:38:14.759
<i>a strange little smile on his face.</i>

563
00:38:14.842 --> 00:38:16.928
<i>watching us drink his beer.</i>

564
00:38:23.684 --> 00:38:25.228
Hey...

565
00:38:25.311 --> 00:38:26.604
Want a cold one. Andy?

566
00:38:26.687 --> 00:38:28.356
No. thanks.

567
00:38:28.439 --> 00:38:30.233
I gave up drinking.

568
00:38:34.070 --> 00:38:37.240
<i>RED: You could argue
he'd done it to curry favor with the guards.</i>

569
00:38:37.323 --> 00:38:40.159
<i>Or maybe make a few friends among us cons.</i>

570
00:38:42.411 --> 00:38:45.874
<i>Me. I think he did it just to feel normal again.</i>

571
00:38:46.959 --> 00:38:49.044
<i>If only for a short while.</i>

572
00:38:51.922 --> 00:38:54.257
RED: King me.

573
00:38:54.341 --> 00:38:56.760
Chess. Now. there's a game of kings.

574
00:38:56.843 --> 00:38:59.429
- What?
- Civilized. strategic.

575
00:38:59.513 --> 00:39:01.848
And a total fucking mystery.

576
00:39:01.932 --> 00:39:03.141
I hate it.

577
00:39:03.225 --> 00:39:05.143
Maybe you'll let me teach you someday.

578
00:39:05.227 --> 00:39:07.688
(CHUCKLES) Yeah. Sure.

579
00:39:07.771 --> 00:39:09.898
I thought of getting a board together.

580
00:39:09.982 --> 00:39:12.776
Well. I'm the right man.
I'm the guy that can get things.

581
00:39:12.859 --> 00:39:14.361
We might do business on a board.

582
00:39:14.444 --> 00:39:17.239
But I want to carve the pieces myself.

583
00:39:17.322 --> 00:39:20.909
One side in alabaster. the opposing side
in soapstone. What do you think?

584
00:39:20.993 --> 00:39:22.912
I think it'll take years.

585
00:39:22.996 --> 00:39:25.581
Well. years I got.
What I don't have are the rocks.

586
00:39:25.665 --> 00:39:27.834
Pickings are pretty slim in the yard.

587
00:39:27.917 --> 00:39:29.877
Pebbles. mostly.

588
00:39:34.215 --> 00:39:37.093
Andy. we're getting to be kinda friends.
aren't we?

589
00:39:37.176 --> 00:39:38.845
Yeah. I guess.

590
00:39:39.971 --> 00:39:42.390
Can I ask you something?

591
00:39:42.473 --> 00:39:44.475
Why did you do it?

592
00:39:45.685 --> 00:39:49.564
I'm innocent. Red. Just like everybody else here.

593
00:39:53.901 --> 00:39:55.987
What are you in for?

594
00:39:57.989 --> 00:39:58.990
Murder.

595
00:39:59.073 --> 00:40:00.492
Same as you.

596
00:40:00.576 --> 00:40:01.744
Innocent?

597
00:40:04.413 --> 00:40:06.498
Only guilty man in Shawshank.

598
00:41:06.017 --> 00:41:07.602
<i>Where's the canary?</i>

599
00:41:07.685 --> 00:41:09.020
<i>How did you know?</i>

600
00:41:09.020 --> 00:41:10.188
<i>How did I know what?</i>

601
00:41:10.271 --> 00:41:12.690
<i>So. you don't know? Come.</i>

602
00:41:15.444 --> 00:41:17.571
<i>This is where the canary is. Johnny.</i>

603
00:41:17.655 --> 00:41:19.865
<i>(WOMAN SINGING)</i>

604
00:41:19.949 --> 00:41:23.953
<i>Quite a surprise to hear a woman singing
in my house. eh. Johnny?</i>

605
00:41:26.872 --> 00:41:29.250
<i>That's quite a...</i>

606
00:41:29.333 --> 00:41:31.001
<i>surprise.</i>

607
00:41:33.504 --> 00:41:35.214
Red...

608
00:41:35.297 --> 00:41:37.091
Wait. wait. wait. wait.

609
00:41:37.174 --> 00:41:39.009
Here she comes.

610
00:41:39.093 --> 00:41:41.971
This is the part I like -
when she does that shit with her hair.

611
00:41:42.054 --> 00:41:45.015
Yeah. I know. I've seen it three times this month.

612
00:41:45.099 --> 00:41:47.101
<i>Gilda. are you decent?</i>

613
00:41:47.184 --> 00:41:49.228
<i>Me?</i>

614
00:41:49.311 --> 00:41:50.854
(MEN HOWL)

615
00:41:50.938 --> 00:41:52.982
Hah! God. I love it!

616
00:41:53.065 --> 00:41:55.151
(MEN CHEER AND WHISTLE)

617
00:41:58.863 --> 00:42:02.659
I understand
you're a man that knows how to get things.

618
00:42:02.742 --> 00:42:06.621
Yeah. I'm known to locate certain things
from time to time. What do you want?

619
00:42:06.705 --> 00:42:09.207
- Rita Hayworth.
- What?

620
00:42:09.291 --> 00:42:11.126
Can you get her?

621
00:42:12.210 --> 00:42:15.672
<i>So. this is Johnny Farrell.
I've heard a lot about you. Johnny.</i>

622
00:42:15.755 --> 00:42:17.632
Take a few weeks.

623
00:42:17.716 --> 00:42:18.925
Weeks?

624
00:42:18.925 --> 00:42:24.180
Well. yeah. Andy. I don't have her stuffed down
the front of my pants right now. I'm sorry to say.

625
00:42:24.264 --> 00:42:26.766
But I'll get her. Relax.

626
00:42:29.019 --> 00:42:30.520
Thanks.

627
00:42:30.603 --> 00:42:32.606
<i>- It was a surprise. Mr. Farrell.
- It was.</i>

628
00:42:32.690 --> 00:42:33.733
MEN: Sit down.

629
00:42:33.816 --> 00:42:35.735
<i>Did you tell him what I'm doing here?</i>

630
00:42:35.818 --> 00:42:38.654
<i>No. I wanted to save that as a surprise. too.</i>

631
00:42:38.738 --> 00:42:40.406
<i>Hang on to your hat. Mr. Farrell...</i>

632
00:42:43.993 --> 00:42:45.703
(GRUNTS)

633
00:42:50.541 --> 00:42:52.793
- Take a walk.
- I gotta change the reel.

634
00:42:52.877 --> 00:42:54.962
I said fuck off!

635
00:42:57.256 --> 00:42:59.717
(DOOR CLOSES AS FILM DIALOGUE CONTINUES)

636
00:42:59.800 --> 00:43:02.595
Ain't you gonna scream?

637
00:43:03.971 --> 00:43:05.765
Let's get this over with.

638
00:43:09.895 --> 00:43:11.521
He broke my fuckin' nose!

639
00:43:22.032 --> 00:43:26.077
Now...I'm gonna open my fly...

640
00:43:26.161 --> 00:43:29.122
and you're gonna swallow
what I give you to swallow.

641
00:43:30.207 --> 00:43:32.000
Then you're gonna swallow Rooster's.

642
00:43:32.083 --> 00:43:34.961
You broke his nose.
he ought to have something to show for it.

643
00:43:35.045 --> 00:43:37.255
Anything you put in my mouth.
you're gonna lose.

644
00:43:37.339 --> 00:43:40.091
No. You don't understand.

645
00:43:40.175 --> 00:43:43.470
You do that. and I'll put
all eight inches of this steel in your ear.

646
00:43:43.470 --> 00:43:44.554
